While his notation was not as modern as today’s algebra symbols, he 
used symbols and abbreviations to simplify complex calculations. 
This early system, sometimes called syncopated algebra, predates 
modern algebraic symbols and provides a structured way to represent 
unknowns, powers, and operations. It focused on rational rather than 
integer solutions, although it inspired the broader development of
Diophantine equations studied today.

Core Features of Diophantine Symbolic Notation
  • Abbreviated symbolic system (not fully symbolic algebra).
  • Unknowns, powers, and coefficients encoded via Greek ciphers.
  • Addition and multiplication handled implicitly by placement.
  • Negative numbers treated as “lacking” quantities.
  • Influenced Arabic algebra and later European symbolic notation.
  • Provided structural groundwork for modern algebra and the
    study of Diophantine equations.

  Pattern:
      POWER SYMBOL  +  UNKNOWN SYMBOL

  Unknown:
      Represented by Greek letters derived from “arithmos.”
      Common unknown symbol:  ς

  Square (x^2):
      ΔΥ   (from *dunamis*, “power/strength”)

  Cube (x^3):
      KΥ   (from *kubos*, “cube”)

  Higher powers:
      ΔΥΔ   (square-square)
      ΔKΥ   (square-cube)
      KΥK   (cube-cube)

Unknowns:
Represented by Greek letters, often contractions of the word 
arithmos ("number"). For example, the unknown symbol is ς (ς).

Powers of the Unknown:
Second power (square): ΔΥ, from Greek dunamis (strength/power)
Cube: KΥ, from Greek kubos (cube)

Higher powers used similar conventions, e.g. ΔΥΔ (square-
square), ΔKΥ (square-cube), KΥK (cube-cube)

Coefficients:
Coefficients are written after the variable (opposite of
modern notation). For constants, symbol M^∘ (M) was used (Greek
monades, units)

  Coefficients follow the variable (reverse of modern notation).

  Constant term:
      M°   (from *monades*, “units”)

Operations:

Addition: Denoted by juxtaposition; terms written consecutively 
are added. Example: αβ represents α+β

Subtraction: Represented by a specialized symbol, an inverted 
capital lambda with a superimposed iota (ΛI), derived from 
Greek leipis ("lacking"). 

Multiplication & Division: Explicit multiplication symbols 
were omitted; division handled by fractions with notation 
like ’νμρ’ιω indicating "divided by".

  Addition:
      Juxtaposition. Writing terms consecutively means “add.”
